l+delivery+tax).\"\n :branches \"Tier selection by quantity (<=100, <=500, >500); rush vs standard; member vs nonmember; free-delivery threshold check\"\n :operators \"pricing compound holds numeric rule parameters but no computation network. struct/slot for output construction. select for branching. +,-,*,/ for arithmetic. min/max for caps. >= for threshold. round-money for final rounding. ttms-premise-closure to attach shared-rule. No existing callable implements the full quote formula.\"\n :operator \"construct a network (e.g. compute-quote) taking quantity, rush?, member?, discount-cap and returning a struct with :production :rush :discount :subtotal :delivery :tax :total\"\n :tests \"Verify production for 80 copies equals 80*2=160 (all in tier-one). Verify rush=0 for standard. Verify discount=0 for nonmember. Verify subtotal=160+25=185. Verify delivery=12 since 185<250. Verify tax=(185+12)*0.08=15.76. Verify total=round-money(212.76)=212.76. Cross-check by computing total independently: 160+0+0+25+12+15.76=212.76. Also test tier boundary: 100 copies should give 200 production; 101 should give 200+1.60=201.60. Test member discount cap: if discount would exceed cap, use cap.\"\n :decision :construct}\n\n## errors\n[]\n\n## role-cell\nWork carefully from the problem and available facts. Follow the strict procedure and the response format for the current phase. The implementation source is admitted into one persistent lexical child.\n\n## procedure-cell\nStrict procedure, used on its own in this experiment. ANALYSIS phase: First inspect errors, the current structured diagnostic snapshot from earlier implementations. Explain relevant diagnostics and any intended repair in :tests and :operators. Unknown alone does not prove an error. Source references identify actions to inspect, not necessarily causes. Runtime input edges and supports are observations, not proof of causality. You may repair earlier work in the next implementation, and should verify both earlier and 